  "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and its magnitude $\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\u2016:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{4}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{4}))j \\\\  & =-4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{4})\\,i+4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{4})\\,j \\\\ \u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\u2016 & =\\sqrt{{[-4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{4})]}^{2}+{[4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{4})]}^{2}} \\\\  & =4|t{|}^{3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}\u2061({t}^{4})+{cos}^{2}\u2061({t}^{4})} \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(u)\u2016\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}4{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $s={t}^{4}$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4} & =s \\\\ t & =\\sqrt[4]{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt[4]{s}) \\\\  & =cos\\,((\\sqrt[4]{s})^{4})\\,i+sin\\,((\\sqrt[4]{s})^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(s)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(s)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
